# usb_hub: a full-speed USB 2.0 **hub** — the class every operating system
# already has a driver for, because a hub is how it finds anything else.
# Three modules: `usb_hub_req` answers the hub and port class requests of
# USB 2.0 §11.24.2 on `usb_ctrl_ep`'s class hook and holds the port state
# they act on, `usb_hub` is that plus the one interface and the one
# interrupt IN endpoint §11.23.1 allows a hub — and no bulk endpoint at all,
# which is what `DATA_ENDP = 4'd0` into `usb_dev_core` means — and the two
# `_fs` / `_ulpi` wrappers put it behind each of this library's link layers.
#
# **It is a hub's control endpoint and not a hub.** A hub also repeats every
# downstream packet to its enabled ports within about four bit times, and
# through a ULPI transceiver the floor is roughly twenty-four one way, so a
# transparent repeater cannot meet USB's turnaround at all. What is behind
# this block's port instead is a second USB controller — `ip/usb_host_ulpi`
# — and joining the two conversations is **`ip/usb_proxy`**, a transaction
# proxy with NAK as the escape hatch. On its own, this block finds itself a
# host that resets its port and gets no answer from the device it believes is
# there; README.md §2 is the timing argument and §8 is the kernel log of that
# outcome.
#
# `port_reset` and `port_reset_done` are the one thing the proxy needs from
# this half: a reset that reaches the real device. Tie `port_reset_done` high
# for a design with nothing downstream and the reset is over in the cycle it
# is asked for, which is what this block did before the proxy existed.
#
# `top` names the ULPI wrapper because that is the one that has run on a
# board; `usb_hub_fs` is the same class layer on a board whose D+ / D- reach
# the FPGA, and `usb_hub` on its own is for a design that has a link layer
# of its own.
#
# Two `depends` lines and they are not alternatives: `usb_device_fs` is
# where `usb_dev_core`, `usb_ctrl_ep`, `usb_bulk_ep`, `usb_pkt_rx` and the
# full-speed line layer live, and `usb_device_ulpi` is where
# `usb_ulpi_link` does. A design that uses only one of the wrappers carries
# the other's link layer in its source list and instantiates nothing from it.
#
# README.md states the descriptor set and the hub descriptor field by field
# with the section each field comes from, says which port features are
# honoured and which are accepted and ignored, and separates what was
# **checked** against a host from what is only **quoted** from the
# specification.
name usb_hub
version 1.0.0
license MIT
description "USB 2.0 full-speed hub: the hub and port class requests of USB 2.0 section 11.24.2, the hub descriptor, one interrupt IN status-change endpoint reporting a bitmap of sticky change bits a host must clear, and a port whose state is read from a second USB controller on the other side of the die"

top usb_hub_ulpi
target ice40
target ecp5

source rtl/usb_hub_req.v
source rtl/usb_hub.v
source rtl/usb_hub_fs.v
source rtl/usb_hub_ulpi.v

depends usb_device_fs ^1.0.0
depends usb_device_ulpi ^1.0.0

param VID bits 16'h1209
param PID bits 16'h0001
param RESET_CYCLES int 300 2..65535
param SE0_CYCLES int 150 2..255
param LINE_TRIES int 40000 1..1000000
param TURNAROUND bits 7'd9
param VENDOR_ADDR bits 6'h00
param VENDOR_DATA bits 8'h00
param CFG_ATTR bits 8'h80
param CFG_POWER bits 8'd50
param MAXPKT0 bits 7'd64
param BUF_RAM int 1 0..1

port clk60 in
port rst_n in
port ulpi_data_i in 8
port ulpi_data_o out 8
port ulpi_data_oe out
port ulpi_dir in
port ulpi_nxt in
port ulpi_stp out
port ulpi_rst_n out
port address out 7
port configured out
port usb_reset out
port phy_ready out
port port_attached in
port port_low_speed in
port port_power out
port port_enabled out
port port_suspended out
port port_reset out
port port_reset_done in
